@integrations/skills/sigil/compilation-execution.sigil import { SigilCompilationExecution }
@integrations/skills/sigil/design-conversation.sigil import { SigilDesignConversation }
@integrations/skills/sigil/review-gate.sigil import { SigilReviewGate }

component SigilDesignCompilationReview {
  goal {
    Consume compiler-owned semantic-readiness and architecture evaluation as
    provisional evidence for exact written Sigil without transferring design
    decisions or implementation approval authority to the compiler.
  }

  interface {
DesignCompilationReview {
      evaluates exact written Sigil through SigilCompilationExecution with design
      focus before user file review and any implementation ReviewGate

      consumes deterministic-foundation, semantic-readiness, and architecture-design
      results from the exact written scope

      requires an exited compiler process and its readable completed Markdown report
      before written-file review, concept grouping, glossary extraction,
      implementation approval, or implementation; this gate cannot be skipped or
      overridden

      permits written-file review when design compilation is green or when every
      yellow finding is explicitly reviewed and accepted as nonblocking by the human
      user
    }
  }
}

expand SigilDesignCompilationReview {
  state {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Design compilation is awaiting, completed-green, completed-yellow,
      completed-red, failed, cancelled, or host-transport-blocked for one exact
      written scope and reviewed boundary. These states map directly to the completed
      Markdown report, failure, cancellation, and host outcomes from
      SigilCompilationExecution; missing completed-report evidence remains
      host-transport-blocked rather than becoming a separate state.
    }
  }

  logic {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Accept the exact scoped Sigil already written by SigilAuthoringWorkflow after
      design intent and applicable external guidance are sufficiently resolved, then
      compile it with SigilCompilationExecution with focus design.

      The affected semantic-unit set is the changed declarations and matching expands
      in the written files, plus direct imported identities named by those units;
      freeze that set with the written-scope evidence before target selection. Derive
      one affected directory from every affected semantic unit's source-file parent,
      including an expand-only or declarationless file. For each candidate, eligible
      candidates are importing `_module.sigil` files within the selected workspace
      whose retrieval closure contains the affected units, followed by exact-case
      components and matching expands in the selected workspace. The workspace target
      is the final fallback when no candidate covers the boundary. count the segments
      in the normalized relative path from each affected directory to each candidate
      declaration or matching-expand directory, sort all resulting vectors lexically
      with smaller counts first, and use that sorted list as the candidate distance
      vector. Select the importing module index whose retrieval closure covers all
      affected semantic units and has the best vector; resolve equal vectors by
      normalized repository-relative module- index path in lexical order. When none
      exists, select the component whose closure maximizes the lexicographic tuple of
      covered semantic units, matching expands, and direct relationships; resolve
      equal tuples with the same distance vector against every candidate declaration
      and matching expand directory, then by normalized source path and exact-case
      component name in lexical order. Compile the workspace without a component when
      no component covers the boundary, and record every unit outside a partial
      target closure.

      Do not use a compiler session for normal authoring or review. Delegate
      invocation, Markdown output isolation, process waiting, retry, and host-failure
      classification entirely to SigilCompilationExecution. Interpret only its
      returned completed report or failure outcome; a running or incomplete execution
      is a hard blocker.

      Use red and yellow diagnostics to investigate evidence and return confirmed
      material problems to DesignConversation. Revise red candidates and yellow
      candidates with unresolved findings.

      Return either a deterministic or coherence correction requirement to the host
      workflow when established intent determines it, or a DesignConversation route
      when the report exposes a material decision, conflicting intent, or future-
      facing pitfall. The host workflow owns correction writes and repetition. Do not
      continue to grouping, glossary extraction, or implementation from red,
      unresolved yellow, unavailable, or incomplete evidence.

      Present the exact green written scope, or a yellow scope whose every finding
      the human explicitly accepted as nonblocking, for user review in the file. Then
      continue to glossary extraction or implementation review.
    }
  }

  constraints {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Compiler green and reviewed yellow are provisional design evidence and do not
      approve glossary mutation or implementation.

      The skill does not duplicate semantic-readiness or architecture-design judgment
      already owned by the compiler focus closure.

      External-guidance applicability, design decisions, correction conversations,
      concept-grouping approval, glossary extraction, and ReviewGate remain owned by
      the host workflow.

      An unavailable, incomplete, or red required design compilation prevents
      implementation review. Yellow also prevents implementation readiness until the
      human explicitly reviews every finding and accepts it as nonblocking.

      A running, unavailable, incomplete, failed, or cancelled required compilation
      is a hard blocker and cannot be waived or reported as a skipped check.

      A report that requires user judgment remains in DesignConversation; the host
      does not guess a correction merely to complete the compile-and-resolve loop.

      Written-file review evidence identifies the target workspace root, target
      paths, changed semantic units, target, profile, focus, and finding
      dispositions. It supports implementation review but never approves it.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilerDrivenDesignReview {
      Decision: Use SigilCompiler focus design as the semantic-readiness and
      modularity evaluator for exact written Sigil.

      Scope: Governs SigilSkill design review after scoped Sigil is written; excludes
      human design decisions, external-guidance research, glossary extraction, and
      implementation approval.

      Trade-offs: Semantic work is immediately visible in the target file, so users
      review the actual change rather than a chat proposal, but compilation may take
      substantial time before it yields a completed report artifact.

      Design issues addressed: Temporary proposal sessions consume context and make
      small-model review harder to follow.

      Discarded alternatives: Retaining duplicate host semantic and modularity review
      was rejected because the compiler design closure already owns those
      evaluations.

      Consequences: One ordinary compiler report evaluates deterministic validity,
      semantic readiness, and architecture on the file the user reviews.
    }

    ReviewedYellowDesignEvidence {
      Decision: Permit a complete yellow design compilation to proceed only after the
      human explicitly reviews and accepts every finding as nonblocking.

      Scope: Governs provisional design evidence for Sigil proposal, glossary, and
      implementation workflows; excludes unavailable, incomplete, and red reports.

      Trade-offs: Noncritical warnings may remain intentionally deferred, and their
      dispositions must travel with the exact report evidence.

      Design issues addressed: Treating every warning as blocking prevents informed
      human judgment, while treating yellow as automatic readiness hides unresolved
      findings.

      Discarded alternatives: Automatic rejection and automatic acceptance of every
      yellow report were rejected.

      Consequences: Reviewed warnings remain visible and scoped to one report; new or
      changed findings require another disposition.
    }

    DeterministicTargetSelection {
      Decision: Rank compile targets by the worst-first vector of normalized path
      distances from every affected semantic unit and lexical identities.

      Scope: Governs ordinary design-compilation target selection; excludes compiler
      status computation, report recovery owned by SigilCompilationExecution, and
      user acceptance of yellow findings.

      Trade-offs: Worst-first path ordering can differ from a host's informal sense
      of proximity.

      Design issues addressed: Undefined proximity let hosts select different design
      closures.

      Discarded alternatives: Host-defined proximity was rejected because it would
      select different design closures for the same written scope.

      Consequences: A target is repeatable across single- and multi-file scopes;
      completed-report recovery remains centralized in SigilCompilationExecution.
    }
  }

  cases {
DesignCompilationReview {
      A red written Sigil with contradictory state ownership returns to
      DesignConversation before concept grouping or glossary extraction.

      A yellow written scope with one unresolved warning remains blocked. When a
      human explicitly accepts every finding as nonblocking, its report and
      dispositions may proceed as provisional evidence.

      An ungrouped green written scope proceeds to concept grouping, then recompiles
      before glossary extraction.

      A host-transport-blocked compilation reports the host-transport evidence and
      remains blocked under SigilCompilationExecution's retry policy.

      A failed compilation reports its failure evidence and remains blocked under the
      execution component's retry policy.

      A cancelled compilation reports its cancellation evidence and exit status and
      blocks review; retry behavior remains owned by SigilCompilationExecution.
    }
  }
}
